-- ============================================================================
-- LEO BUSINESS ADVISORY — Production Initial Setup
--
-- Generated by concatenating supabase/migrations/0001_init_schema.sql
-- through 0010_storage_size_limit.sql, in that exact numeric order, with
-- no SQL statement added, removed, or altered — every table, column, RLS
-- policy, function, trigger, and Storage policy below is byte-identical
-- in meaning to its source migration file. Section dividers below name
-- the source file each block came from, purely for traceability back to
-- supabase/migrations/.
--
-- INTENDED USE: apply this ONCE, in full, top to bottom, in the Supabase
-- SQL Editor of a brand-new project that has none of these objects yet.
-- It is NOT safe to run a second time against the same project — most
-- `create policy` statements below have no `if not exists` guard (Postgres
-- doesn't support one for policies), matching the original migration
-- files exactly; a second run will fail the first time it hits an
-- already-existing policy. This mirrors the source migrations' own
-- behavior — no duplicate-execution guard has been added here that
-- wasn't already present in a given file.
--
-- AFTER running this file, one manual step still has to happen outside
-- SQL (see supabase/README.md and 0002's own header comment below): no
-- one has admin access yet. Create your admin login in the Supabase
-- Dashboard (Authentication → Users → Add user), copy that user's UUID,
-- then run this once, by itself, with the real UUID substituted in:
--
--   insert into admin_users (user_id) values ('<uuid from auth.users>');
--
-- Until that insert runs, RLS on every content table denies all writes
-- to everyone (including a logged-in user) — this is expected, not a
-- bug: public read continues to work throughout.
-- ============================================================================


